      { q: "Distinguish between ’could’ and ’was able to’ with examples.", a: "Could is used for general ability in the past: ’She could swim at the age of six.’\nWas able to is used for one particular achievement in a specific situation: ’The firemen were able to rescue everyone from the burning house.’", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Rewrite using a modal without changing the meaning:\n(a) It was wrong of us not to inform the police.\n(b) Perhaps the flight is delayed.", a: "(a) We should have informed the police. (or ought to have informed)\n(b) The flight may (or might) be delayed. (possibility)", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Correct the errors and give the reason:\n(a) You should better consult a lawyer.\n(b) She didn’t ought to have shouted at the child.", a: "(a) You had better consult a lawyer (or You should consult a lawyer). Reason: ’had better’ is the fixed form; ’should better’ is wrong.\n(b) She ought not to have shouted at the child (or She shouldn’t have shouted). Reason: ought is negated with ’not’, not with did/didn’t.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State the meaning expressed by the modal in each (obligation, deduction, ability, prohibition, regret):\n(a) The lights are on, so she must be at home.\n(b) You mustn’t park here.\n(c) We should have booked the tickets earlier.", a: "(a) Deduction: a logical conclusion from evidence.\n(b) Prohibition: parking here is not allowed.\n(c) Regret or criticism: the right action was not taken in the past.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Fill in suitable modals and give the sense:\n(a) I’d love to come, but I ___ finish my project first.\n(b) ___ I borrow your notes for a day?\n(c) You ___ worried; the exam was easy and I passed.", a: "(a) have to (or must): obligation.\n(b) May (or Could): polite request for permission.\n(c) needn’t have: the worry was unnecessary, though it happened.", marks: 3 }
